WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.833
- [Instructor] What's not to love

00:00:00.833 --> 00:00:02.320
when it comes to croissants?

00:00:02.320 --> 00:00:05.630
Flaky on the outside, soft
and buttery on the inside,

00:00:05.630 --> 00:00:08.320
they might just be our
favorite French pastry.

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:11.250
Though easy to enjoy, croissants
can be difficult to perfect

00:00:11.250 --> 00:00:13.260
especially if you're making them at home.

00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:16.440
The process takes practice
and a lot of patience,

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:18.800
but trust us, when we say it's worth it.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:21.100
We tested hundreds of croissants

00:00:21.100 --> 00:00:23.420
to bring you the best classic croissant

00:00:23.420 --> 00:00:25.130
and have you baking like a pro.

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.034
So let's get into it.

00:00:27.034 --> 00:00:29.701
(gentle music)

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:33.150
We're gonna start by making our dough.

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:35.440
Our dough consists of eight ingredients.

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:39.070
Milk, water, flour, sugar,

00:00:39.070 --> 00:00:42.140
salt, yeast, malt, and butter.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.670
In a large bowl combine
the milk and water.

00:00:44.670 --> 00:00:47.020
A lot of croissant recipes
call for just water,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.780
but we found that a combination
of water and whole milk

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:51.840
creates that tender crumb we want.

00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.190
The added sugar from the milk
will also help us achieve

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:55.970
that deep copper color.

00:00:55.970 --> 00:00:57.830
Okay, next, we'll add the flour.

00:00:57.830 --> 00:01:00.160
We're using bread flour
because it's high in protein

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:02.390
and one of the strongest flours available.

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:04.240
It's gonna help provide
the structural support

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:05.073
for our dough.

00:01:05.073 --> 00:01:08.213
The extra protein in bread
flour makes for better volume,

00:01:08.213 --> 00:01:11.090
chewier crumb, and more
browning in the crust.

00:01:11.090 --> 00:01:14.830
Now we'll add our sugar, salt, yeast,

00:01:14.830 --> 00:01:16.780
and malt for extra flavor.

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:19.690
The last ingredient for our
dough is unsalted butter.

00:01:19.690 --> 00:01:22.080
Not all croissant recipes
use butter in the dough,

00:01:22.080 --> 00:01:24.270
but we found that a small
amount of butter fat

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.850
in the dough helps during
the lamination process.

00:01:26.850 --> 00:01:28.430
We'll mix this together with our hands

00:01:28.430 --> 00:01:30.510
until the dough resembles a shaggy mass.

00:01:30.510 --> 00:01:32.350
We don't wanna develop gluten here,

00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:35.530
we just wanna hydrate the flour
and create a unified dough.

00:01:35.530 --> 00:01:36.930
Trust your instincts here.

00:01:36.930 --> 00:01:39.290
If your dough feels too wet, like this,

00:01:39.290 --> 00:01:40.910
it'll be more difficult to roll out

00:01:40.910 --> 00:01:43.540
which will cause the butter
to shatter laminating.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:45.780
You can always add a
little bit more flour here.

00:01:45.780 --> 00:01:48.860
If your dough is too dry,
it'll begin tear during rolling

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:50.950
and eventually bake into
something more similar

00:01:50.950 --> 00:01:52.580
to a bread roll than a pastry.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.800
Unfortunately, there's
really no fix for this.

00:01:54.800 --> 00:01:57.060
Cover the bowl with plastic
wrap or a kitchen towel

00:01:57.060 --> 00:01:59.110
and allow the dough to
rest at room temperature

00:01:59.110 --> 00:02:01.540
until it rises to about
one and a half times

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:04.800
its original size and has
a slightly puffy texture.

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:06.600
This will probably take
about an hour depending

00:02:06.600 --> 00:02:07.880
on the temperature of your kitchen.

00:02:07.880 --> 00:02:09.850
Okay, this looks good.

00:02:09.850 --> 00:02:11.080
We'll punch down the dough

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:13.120
and transfer it onto a
piece of plastic wrap.

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:16.630
Then gently press the dough
into a rough eight inch square.

00:02:16.630 --> 00:02:17.500
There we go.

00:02:17.500 --> 00:02:19.640
Then we'll wrap the dough
tightly and refrigerate

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:22.480
for at least three hours,
but preferably overnight.

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:24.770
Let's move on to one of
the most crucial steps

00:02:24.770 --> 00:02:27.740
of croissants, the
butter block or beurrage.

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:30.100
It's really important to
use a high quality butter.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.270
We recommend using European
or European-style butter

00:02:33.270 --> 00:02:36.200
because we're looking for
at least 84% butter fat.

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:37.520
That's gonna help give our croissants

00:02:37.520 --> 00:02:39.440
that famous honeycomb finish.

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:42.530
To make the butter block place
a sheet of parchment paper

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:45.180
on a work surface with the
long side nearest to you.

00:02:45.180 --> 00:02:47.950
Next we'll slice the butter
into 1/4 inch thick pieces

00:02:47.950 --> 00:02:51.370
and arrange the slices in
a single layer like tiles.

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:53.980
Fold the edges of the parchment
paper to encase the butter

00:02:53.980 --> 00:02:55.260
and flip the packet over.

00:02:55.260 --> 00:02:57.030
So the seam is facing down.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.180
We'll let the butter packet
rest at room temperature

00:02:59.180 --> 00:03:01.250
until it's soft but not greasy.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:03.990
Chilled yet pliable is what
most recipes will tell you.

00:03:03.990 --> 00:03:05.100
We essentially want the butter

00:03:05.100 --> 00:03:07.690
to be a little bendy but not break.

00:03:07.690 --> 00:03:09.530
For us it took about 30 minutes

00:03:09.530 --> 00:03:11.550
for our butter to reach this consistency.

00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:14.300
Then we'll take a rolling pin
and roll it over the packet

00:03:14.300 --> 00:03:17.070
to distribute the butter in
an even layer all the way

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:18.570
to the edges and corners,

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.950
leaving no spaces between the tiles.

00:03:20.950 --> 00:03:22.060
And there you have it.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:24.520
Place the butter packet in
the refrigerator to chill

00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:25.980
until you're ready to laminate.

00:03:25.980 --> 00:03:27.770
All right, let's laminate.

00:03:27.770 --> 00:03:31.010
First thing we need to do is
lock the butter into the dough.

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.310
To do this we wanna make
sure that our butter

00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:36.600
and our dough are about the
same temperature and texture.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.320
Simply hold the butter in one hand

00:03:38.320 --> 00:03:40.630
and the dough in the
other hand and feel them.

00:03:40.630 --> 00:03:43.440
We'll dust our work surface
very lightly with flour.

00:03:43.440 --> 00:03:45.000
We will use our butter block

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.890
as the guide to how large
we roll out our dough.

00:03:47.890 --> 00:03:50.560
We wanna roll out our dough
to be about the same width

00:03:50.560 --> 00:03:53.170
and about twice the height
of our butter packet.

00:03:53.170 --> 00:03:54.610
Once the dough is rolled out,

00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:56.650
place the packet butter side down

00:03:56.650 --> 00:03:58.290
on the bottom half of the dough,

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:01.080
leaving about a half inches
space around the edges.

00:04:01.080 --> 00:04:03.110
Once you have the butter
in the correct spot,

00:04:03.110 --> 00:04:05.070
carefully peel back the parchment paper

00:04:05.070 --> 00:04:06.260
to release the butter.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:09.430
Now fold the top half of
the dough over the butter,

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:11.820
stretching it ever so
gently so that the edges

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:15.010
of the dough reaches the exposed
edges beneath the butter.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:17.560
Use the palm of your hand
to gently press the side

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:19.160
and bottom edges of the dough together

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:20.970
to seal the packet closed.

00:04:20.970 --> 00:04:21.840
Look at this!

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.080
Our dough looks sort
of like a closed book.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.670
So moving forward, we'll
call this the spine side

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.210
and this will be the page side.

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:30.770
We wanna make sure that the
butter rolls out evenly.

00:04:30.770 --> 00:04:33.510
So we'll use our rolling pin
to firmly press the dough

00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:35.160
and begin distributing the butter.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.410
To release that tension from
folding the dough onto itself,

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:41.590
we'll use a rated knife to cut
down the middle of the spine.

00:04:41.590 --> 00:04:43.510
Now make sure to orient the dough so

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:46.950
that the split spine is closest
to you and begin rolling.

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:49.810
Continue rolling until the
dough is about 12 inches high,

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:52.300
fluffing and moving the dough
to make sure it doesn't stick

00:04:52.300 --> 00:04:53.400
to our work surface.

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:55.440
Once we have the height,
we wanna roll the dough

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:57.440
until it's about a 1/4 inch thick.

00:04:57.440 --> 00:04:59.590
It's time to make our first fold.

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:01.490
Fold the bottom third of the dough up

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:03.340
and the top third of the dough down,

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:04.710
kind of like folding a letter.

00:05:04.710 --> 00:05:07.350
Hence why we call this fold a letter fold.

00:05:07.350 --> 00:05:08.780
We wanna continue rolling out

00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:11.130
to about the same size
rectangle we had before,

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:12.780
with a 1/4 inch thickness.

00:05:12.780 --> 00:05:14.770
If your rectangle gets a little wild

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:17.830
and has some rounded edges,
you can use a serrated knife

00:05:17.830 --> 00:05:20.310
or a pastry wheel to
straighten out the sides.

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:23.160
We wanna keep as close to a
perfect rectangle as possible

00:05:23.160 --> 00:05:24.400
for optimal layers.

00:05:24.400 --> 00:05:27.210
Now we're ready for our
second and final fold.

00:05:27.210 --> 00:05:28.700
Fold the left two thirds of dough

00:05:28.700 --> 00:05:31.610
over itself to just pass
the center of the dough.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:33.130
Then fold the right third of the dough

00:05:33.130 --> 00:05:36.100
over itself towards the
left so that the edges meet.

00:05:36.100 --> 00:05:38.560
Gently press the two ones
together to make a seam

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:40.620
that resembles an offset book spine.

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:42.330
Then fold the left side of the dough over

00:05:42.330 --> 00:05:44.120
to meet the right edge of the dough.

00:05:44.120 --> 00:05:45.950
This is called a four-fold.

00:05:45.950 --> 00:05:47.690
A lot of recipes will have you resting

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:49.740
and folding the dough three and four times

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:51.520
to create a crazy amount of layers

00:05:51.520 --> 00:05:53.710
and show off that iconic
honeycomb structure.

00:05:53.710 --> 00:05:56.540
However, we found that
although fewer folds

00:05:56.540 --> 00:05:59.570
creates fewer layers,
they're more distinct layers,

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:02.560
and honestly give the croissant
our favorite crumb texture,

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:04.480
light with just enough chew.

00:06:04.480 --> 00:06:07.690
Plus it's a little less labor intensive.

00:06:07.690 --> 00:06:10.400
Our croissants are really shaping up,

00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:13.110
but actually let's get
to shaping these babies.

00:06:13.110 --> 00:06:15.820
Now we gotta roll the
dough out one more time

00:06:15.820 --> 00:06:16.780
and you guessed it,

00:06:16.780 --> 00:06:19.200
get back down to that 1/4 inch thickness.

00:06:19.200 --> 00:06:21.420
For this next part we
like to use a pastry wheel

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:24.230
but you can also totally
use a large chef's knife.

00:06:24.230 --> 00:06:25.900
Begin by marking three inches

00:06:25.900 --> 00:06:28.070
and roll the wheel from
the mark diagonally

00:06:28.070 --> 00:06:29.730
towards the bottom left corner.

00:06:29.730 --> 00:06:31.760
Continue cutting the
rest of the dough until

00:06:31.760 --> 00:06:33.980
you have about 10 to 12 triangles.

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:36.720
Make a slit in the middle
of the base of each triangle

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:38.340
to help relieve some tension

00:06:38.340 --> 00:06:41.350
and then roll them up to create
that iconic crescent shape.

00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:42.590
Make sure to roll as tight

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:45.010
as possible without
applying too much pressure

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:46.770
so that the croissants will hold its shape

00:06:46.770 --> 00:06:48.520
as it proofs and bakes.

00:06:48.520 --> 00:06:50.810
The French word croissant
actually translates

00:06:50.810 --> 00:06:52.500
to crescent in English.

00:06:52.500 --> 00:06:55.380
Many historical experts believe
French croissants evolved

00:06:55.380 --> 00:06:56.850
from Austrian kipferl,

00:06:56.850 --> 00:06:58.890
a buttery and often sweet baked good

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:00.410
shaped like a crescent moon.

00:07:00.410 --> 00:07:05.160
According to the popular legend
kipferl dates back to 1683

00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.510
when a baker heard the
Ottoman Turks tunneling

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:09.600
beneath Vienna to lay siege to it.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.620
He sounded the alarm to save the city

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:14.310
and created kipferl to
celebrate this victory.

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:16.330
It's curve shape supposedly symbolize

00:07:16.330 --> 00:07:18.690
the crescent moon emblem
on the Ottoman flag.

00:07:18.690 --> 00:07:20.960
Kipferl eventually migrated to France

00:07:20.960 --> 00:07:24.150
and evolved into what we now
know and love as the croissant.

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:27.350
Before we get to baking, we
have to proof our pastries.

00:07:27.350 --> 00:07:29.840
We like to add boiling
water to a small vessel

00:07:29.840 --> 00:07:31.460
like a ramekin, and place it on

00:07:31.460 --> 00:07:34.280
the tray under the plastic wrap
while the croissants proof.

00:07:34.280 --> 00:07:37.350
Moisture is key to prevent
the dough from drying out.

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:38.520
You'll know they're ready to bake

00:07:38.520 --> 00:07:40.850
when they look like light puffy clouds.

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:43.290
They should slightly wobble
when you shake the tray.

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:45.230
Lastly, we'll brush them with an egg wash

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:49.100
to give our croissants that
iconic shiny copper-brown shell.

00:07:49.100 --> 00:07:51.440
And finally, it's time to bake.

00:07:51.440 --> 00:07:53.300
We recommend placing a pan filled

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:55.710
with boiling water at
the bottom of your oven.

00:07:55.710 --> 00:07:58.140
Steam ultimately helps the dough grow

00:07:58.140 --> 00:08:00.430
before the heat traps it in it's shell.

00:08:00.430 --> 00:08:03.180
Bake the croissants in
the oven at 350 degrees

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:04.810
for about 30 minutes.

00:08:04.810 --> 00:08:07.010
And before we bite into
these works of perfection,

00:08:07.010 --> 00:08:09.550
we wanna let them cool
for about 20 minutes.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.450
And voila, perfectly golden croissants.

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.910
We think croissants taste
best when baked fresh

00:08:14.910 --> 00:08:17.450
with a smear of jam or a drizzle of honey,

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:20.250
and served with your
favorite breakfast beverage.

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:22.000
The possibilities are really endless

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:23.990
when it comes to using
this laminated dough

00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:26.800
to make other beautiful flaky pastries.

00:08:26.800 --> 00:08:29.090
This recipe is truly a labor of love.

00:08:29.090 --> 00:08:32.410
And once you nail it, the
reward is insanely sweet.

00:08:32.410 --> 00:08:34.710
For this recipe and even
more croissant baking tips,

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:36.520
check out tasty.com.

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:39.684
Croissants really make
the world a butter place.

00:08:39.684 --> 00:08:42.025
(laughing)

00:08:42.025 --> 00:08:44.692
(gentle music)

